c4131263072="" class="response-container-content ng-tns-c4131263072-21 is-mobile"><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c1916596297="" class="response-content ng-tns-c4131263072-21"><!----><thinking-overlay _ngcontent-ng-c1916596297="" _nghost-ng-c3909110362="" class="enable-luminous-fast-follows ng-star-inserted"><!----></thinking-overlay><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c1916596297="" class="model-response-label-announcer" aria-live="off" aria-busy="false"><!----><!----><h2 _ngcontent-ng-c1916596297="" class="cdk-visually-hidden screen-reader-model-response-label ng-star-inserted" style=""> Gemini said </h2><!----><!----></div><!----><structured-content-container _ngcontent-ng-c1916596297="" class="model-response-text is-mobile processing-state-visible ng-star-inserted" _nghost-ng-c1632122541="" style="height: auto;"><div _ngcontent-ng-c1632122541="" class="container"><message-content _ngcontent-ng-c1632122541="" _nghost-ng-c2728394718="" id="message-content-id-r_4bb92c89ef8d0e9d" class="ng-star-inserted"><div _ngcontent-ng-c2728394718="" inline-copy-host="" class="markdown markdown-main-panel enable-luminous-fast-follows enable-updated-hr-color tutor-markdown-rendering" id="model-response-message-contentr_4bb92c89ef8d0e9d" aria-busy="false" aria-live="off" dir="ltr" style="--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out;"><p data-path-to-node="0">It can be incredibly frustrating when you <i data-path-to-node="0" data-index-in-node="42">know</i> your internet is working (like when you are running <code data-path-to-node="0" data-index-in-node="99">npm install</code> or streaming a video), but Windows Task Manager stubbornly shows <b data-path-to-node="0" data-index-in-node="176">0 Mbps</b> across the board.</p><p data-path-to-node="1">On Windows 10, this is a very common glitch. It usually happens because Task Manager is looking at the wrong virtual network adapter or because a driver setting is filtering out the traffic.</p><p data-path-to-node="2">Here is why it happens and how to fix it so you can see your real-time network speeds again.</p><h2 data-path-to-node="4">üîç Why Task Manager Shows 0 Mbps</h2><h3 data-path-to-node="5">1. The Npcap / Wireshark Conflict (Most Common for Devs)</h3><p data-path-to-node="6">As a developer, if you have ever installed <b data-path-to-node="6" data-index-in-node="43">Wireshark</b>, <b data-path-to-node="6" data-index-in-node="54">Npcap</b>, or certain VPNs/emulators, they install a packet-capturing driver. This driver accidentally hijacks how Windows measures network statistics, making Task Manager report absolute zero.</p><h3 data-path-to-node="7">2. Connected to a Virtual Adapter</h3><p data-path-to-node="8">If you use Docker, WSL2 (Windows Subsystem for Linux), or VirtualBox, Windows creates "Virtual Adapters". Task Manager sometimes gets confused and tracks the traffic on an inactive virtual adapter instead of your actual hardware Wi-Fi or Ethernet card.</p><h2 data-path-to-node="10">üõ†Ô∏è How to Fix It</h2><h3 data-path-to-node="11">Fix 1: Change the View in Task Manager</h3><p data-path-to-node="12">Before changing deep settings, check if Task Manager is just hiding the data:</p><ol start="1" data-path-to-node="13"><li><p data-path-to-node="13,0,0">Open <b data-path-to-node="13,0,0" data-index-in-node="5">Task Manager</b> (<code data-path-to-node="13,0,0" data-index-in-node="19">Ctrl + Shift + Esc</code>).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="13,1,0">Go to the <b data-path-to-node="13,1,0" data-index-in-node="10">Performance</b> tab and click on <b data-path-to-node="13,1,0" data-index-in-node="39">Network</b> (Wi-Fi or Ethernet) on the left.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="13,2,0">Right-click anywhere on the big graph, hover over <b data-path-to-node="13,2,0" data-index-in-node="50">View network details</b>, and ensure your primary adapter is selected.</p></li></ol><h3 data-path-to-node="14">Fix 2: Disable the "Network Monitor" Driver Feature</h3><p data-path-to-node="15">If you have packet capture software installed, this quick setting tweak usually brings the numbers back to life:</p><ol start="1" data-path-to-node="16"><li><p data-path-to-node="16,0,0">Press <code data-path-to-node="16,0,0" data-index-in-node="6">Win + R</code>, type <code data-path-to-node="16,0,0" data-index-in-node="20">ncpa.cpl</code>, and hit Enter. (This opens your Network Connections window).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="16,1,0">Right-click on your active internet connection (Wi-Fi or Ethernet) and select <b data-path-to-node="16,1,0" data-index-in-node="78">Properties</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="16,2,0">Look through the list itemized under <i data-path-to-node="16,2,0" data-index-in-node="37">"This connection uses the following items:"</i>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="16,3,0">Look for <b data-path-to-node="16,3,0" data-index-in-node="9">Npcap Packet Driver</b> or <b data-path-to-node="16,3,0" data-index-in-node="32">Network Monitor Driver</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="16,4,0"><b data-path-to-node="16,4,0" data-index-in-node="0">Uncheck</b> it, click <b data-path-to-node="16,4,0" data-index-in-node="18">OK</b>, and restart Task Manager.</p></li></ol><h3 data-path-to-node="17">Fix 3: Reset the Network Stack via Command Prompt</h3><p data-path-to-node="18">Sometimes the Windows network counters just get corrupted. You can reset them using a few commands. Open your Command Prompt as <b data-path-to-node="18" data-index-in-node="128">Administrator</b> and run these commands one by one:</p><response-element class="" ng-version="0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER"><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><code-block _nghost-ng-c2911514074="" class="ng-tns-c2911514074-39 enable-luminous-code-block ng-star-inserted" style=""><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c2911514074="" class="code-block ng-tns-c2911514074-39 ng-animate-disabled ng-trigger ng-trigger-